module Darklang.Cli.Docs.Testing

let content () : String =
  """# Testing Dark code

A test is an ordinary function taking Unit and returning Stdlib.Test.T.
There is nothing to register. Put tests beside your code or in a Tests module.

## Your first test

  module /MyApp.Math - <<'EOF'
  let double (n: Int) : Int = n * 2

  let doubles () : Stdlib.Test.T =
    double 4 |> Stdlib.Test.eq 8
  EOF

  test MyApp.Math
  test MyApp.Math.doubles

Or start with a template:

  test create MyApp.Math.Tests.doubles --for MyApp.Math.double

This creates an ordinary function with a failing TODO and opens $EDITOR when
a terminal is available. --for reads the function's parameters and adds a
table example in the doc comment; you choose the inputs and expected result.
Use --no-editor to create only. Existing items are never overwritten.
Use view, edit, status and commit as you would for any function.

## Writing checks

One check:

  let doublesFour () : Stdlib.Test.T =
    double 4 |> Stdlib.Test.eq 8

Many inputs for one function:

  let doubles () : Stdlib.Test.T =
    Stdlib.Test.table double [ (0, 0), (4, 8), (-3, -6) ]

A row is the inputs, then the expected result. Several inputs are a tuple:

  let xor () : Stdlib.Test.T =
    Stdlib.Test.table Stdlib.Bool.xor
      [ ((true, false), true), ((true, true), false) ]

The function says how many parameters it has, so the tuple is spread across a
function of several and passed whole to a function that takes one tuple.

Several checks in one test:

  let checks () : Stdlib.Test.T =
    Stdlib.Test.all
      [ double 0 |> Stdlib.Test.eq 0,
        double 4 |> Stdlib.Test.eq 8 ]

Code that should raise: name the function, the message, and the inputs.

  let toIntRejectsNaN () : Stdlib.Test.T =
    Stdlib.Test.tableRaises
      Stdlib.Float.toInt
      "Encountered out-of-range value for type of Int"
      [ Stdlib.Float.nan, Stdlib.Float.infinity ]

When each case is a different function, a row is the function then its input:

  Stdlib.Test.eachRaises
    "Encountered out-of-range value for type of Int"
    [ (Stdlib.Float.ceiling, Stdlib.Float.nan),
      (Stdlib.Float.floor, Stdlib.Float.negativeInfinity) ]

Inputs work as in table, so several arguments are a tuple:

  Stdlib.Test.tableRaises Stdlib.Int.divide "Cannot divide by 0" [ (1, 0), (5, 0) ]

For an expression rather than one function call, raisesWith takes a lambda:

  Stdlib.Test.raisesWith
    "Cannot unwrap None"
    (fun () -> Stdlib.List.head [] |> Builtin.unwrap)
    ()

The lambda matters: written without it, the expression would raise before the helper
ran. Most Dark code reports bad input as a Result or an Option; test those with eq.

Floats are compared exactly, with one exception: in a test comparison a NaN is the same
value as a NaN, so a row can expect one: (Stdlib.Float.nan, Stdlib.Float.nan). The ==
operator follows IEEE, where NaN equals nothing. A NaN nested inside an Ok or a tuple is
compared with ==, so check that one with Stdlib.Float.isNaN. Stdlib.Float.nan, infinity and
negativeInfinity are the constants to test with.

Checks return values. A failed check does not throw: return it or include it
in all. Binding a check with `let` and never reading it discards its result,
so the test passes whatever the check said. Saving, `typecheck` and the editor
warn about that (UnusedTestResult). `test` also shows warnings beneath each selected
test's result. Warnings do not change pass/fail results or block a commit.
all [] passes as an empty combination, but an empty table, actualExpected,
tableRaises or eachRaises fails with "No cases supplied".

The last line of a run counts both: `37 passed (166 checks)`. A test is one
function; a check is one row of a table, or one eq or isTrue.

## Finding and running tests

  test                             tests under your active account
  test MyApp.Math                   a module and its descendants
  test MyApp.Math.Tests.doubles     one test by its full name
  test list MyApp                   discover without executing
  test MyApp --filter doubles       match part of the full name
  --branch fix test MyApp           run the branch's version, draft included

Without an active account, specify a module or test name. Bare `test` refuses
to run rather than selecting other owners' tests.

Tab completion offers modules, test names and flags. Tests are listed under their
module, one line each: the name, then the verdict and how many checks it made. The
name is printed before the test starts, so a test that hangs is a name with nothing
after it. Output written by a test is buffered until it finishes, then printed
indented beneath its completed result row. Whatever did not pass is named again at
the end, in full, ready to paste into `test <name>`. PASS means its checks passed;
FAIL means assertions failed; ERROR means execution could not finish normally.
Table errors name their row and input, preserve other failures, and allow later
rows to run. A test containing both failed and errored rows counts as one errored
test.

Exit 0 means all selected tests passed (or a nonempty list); 1 means a failure,
error or discovery problem; 2 means invalid arguments or no tests selected.

Tests run sequentially with the instance's permissions, in this process.
There is no timeout or isolation: writes affect the real store and a sync can
reach the configured relay. Ctrl+C stops a hung run.

## Helper reference

  actual |> Stdlib.Test.eq expected
  check |> Stdlib.Test.withMessage "checking the total"
  Stdlib.Test.table fn [ (input, expected) ]
  Stdlib.Test.all [ firstCheck, secondCheck ]
  Stdlib.Test.actualExpected [ (actual, expected) ]
  Stdlib.Test.tableRaises fn "message" [ input ]
  Stdlib.Test.eachRaises "message" [ (fn, input) ]
  Stdlib.Test.raisesWith "message" fn input
  Stdlib.Test.fail "explanation"
  Stdlib.Test.isTrue value
  Stdlib.Test.isFalse value
  Stdlib.Test.eq actual expected     the same check, as a plain call

actualExpected evaluates its rows before the helper runs;
an error there aborts evaluation. all also receives already evaluated checks.
Use table when calls need error recovery between rows. withMessage prefixes
every assertion failure and execution error in a combined result.

A helper with any other signature is not discovered as a test. Give a helper
an argument if it would otherwise look like a test.
"""
